messages.reasoning_content:
Two issues:
1. `-- vault` → the original gives the "subcommand hint" but mine gives rc=0 (no error) — because after `--`, my parse treats 'vault' as a positional command lookup ✓. I need: after `--`, a positional that matches a command name is NOT a subcommand → the unexpected-arg error with the subcommand hint ✓.

   So in parse(), after `--`, I should NOT dispatch to the command; instead, error with the subcommand hint if the arg matches a command name, else the plain unexpected error ✓.

2. `-- foo` → mine adds the subcommand hint but 'foo' is not a command ✗ → the hint should only apply when the arg matches a command name ✓.

Let me fix parse():
```rust
if after {
    // positional: not a subcommand anymore
    let is_command = COMMANDS.iter().any(|c| c.name == arg || c.aliases.contains(&arg.as_str()));
    return unexpected(arg, &top_usage, is_command);
}
```
Hmm, and the `-h`/`--help` handling: after `--`, no flag handling ✓ (my code already checks `!after` ✓).

Hmm, but careful: after `--`, `--help` becomes a positional → starts with '-' → the dash hint ✓ (observed: `-- --help` → "Found argument '--help'... If you tried to supply `--help` as a value rather than a flag, use `-- --help`" ✓).

So after `--`: if the arg starts with '-' → the dash hint; else if it matches a command → the subcommand hint; else → no hint ✓.

Let me restructure parse():
```rust
loop {
    let arg = &argv[i];
    if !after && arg == "--" { after = true; i += 1; continue; }
    if !after {
        if arg == "-h" || arg == "--help" { return TopHelp }
        if arg.starts_with('-') { return unexpected(arg, &top_usage, false) }
        if let Some(index) = find_command(arg) { return parse_command(...) }
        // unknown command → suggestions or unexpected
        ...
    } else {
        // after --: never a subcommand
        let is_command = ...;
        return unexpected(arg, &top_usage, is_command);
    }
}
```
Hmm, but wait: after `--`, are MULTIPLE args possible? E.g. `jt -- vault foo` → the error would be about 'vault' (the first) ✓ (parse-time).

Hmm, and the suggestion logic doesn't apply after `--` ✓ (observed: `-- nopee` → hmm, untested; let me test).

Let me also test `-- nopee` and `-- vault foo`.

Let me fix and test.

